<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#yakoyaner">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yakoyaner</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Iakoiane, clan mother</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yakoyaner, also spelt Iakoiane, is the Kanien'kehá꞉ka (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a>) term for the clan mother of the Haudenosaunee, the senior woman responsible for the political, economic and social stability of her clan. In this matrilineal society the clan mothers chose the Royaner, the male chiefs, watched over them in council and could remove a chief who led poorly. They also named children, kept the order of the clan's names, adopted new members, among them prisoners of war, and passed on the Creation Story. In the Confederacy's history the first clan mother was Jigonsaseh, who with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-peacemaker">Great Peacemaker</a> created its government around the 12th century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The duties of the clan mother grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-law-of-peace">Great Law of Peace</a> (Kaianere'kó:wa, or Gayanashagowa), the law of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy. Each of the Confederacy's languages has its own word for her: iakoiá꞉ner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a>, goyá·neh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onondaga-people">Onondaga</a>, goyá꞉neh in Cayuga and ukuwanáˀtha in Tuscarora. Kinship, identity, territory and property pass through the female line. Women oversaw the longhouses, communal agriculture and cooking, and served with men as Keepers of Faith. Haudenosaunee creation accounts attribute the discovery of the world to a woman, Sky Woman (Atahensic), who brought seeds with her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The title passes along hereditary lines, first to an oldest sister and, failing her, to the oldest daughter, though a clan mother often chooses and trains her own successor, usually a daughter. To hold it a woman must be married to a man of another clan, know the language of her nation and have daughters; descent alone is not enough. A clan mother who neglects her duties is first warned by her female relatives, and if she does not heed them the matter goes to the chief to confirm her removal.</p><p>The Kanien'kehá:ka see the Yakoyaner as holders of knowledge chosen because they show the good mind, and as a link between past and present. In their teaching women were created first and came from the earth, and the earth is regarded as mother; this view of both the earth and the clan mother as mother underlies their matricentric social and political order.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Beyond choosing and removing chiefs, the clan mother supervises the clan's interests and monitors the public treasury. She makes sure that no child is given the name of a living person and that no couple marries within one clan. Clan mothers prepare the meals of the chiefs in council, a reminder that women, who held the gardens, were the economic source of the community. Female councils took up issues before they were sent to the men's committee.</p><p>European and Haudenosaunee sources indicate that clan mothers shared in declarations of war and often went with the chiefs to Confederacy councils and treaty talks, acting as mediators or appointing a male speaker. They mattered most in negotiations over land, given women's role as cultivators. British officials, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-johnson">Sir William Johnson</a>, reported their influence and tried to win their favour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yakoyaner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
